@article{ref194,
    abbrev_source_title = "UNKNOWN",
    abstract = "This chapter explains how I facilitate students' engagements with   Tableau Public-a free platform to create, share, and explore data   visualizations online-ultimately to produce digital essays that lend   insight into Black literary art and history. Multi-media compositions,   or data storytelling, empowers students from diverse backgrounds to   engage with African American literary art and cultivate a strong   narrative voice, even if they have not had a prior class that focused   solely on Black writing. In my senior seminar class,   \\#TheBeyonceExperience, I use the singer Beyonce as a gateway figure to   interrogate key tenets of Black Feminism and draw parallels between the   famed entertainer and writers such as Nikki Giovanni, Margaret Walker,   and Lucille Clifton. Students draw on quantitative and qualitative   datasets to derive insight about Black literary art and compose   interactive, visual narratives that reveal how Beyonce extends a long   tradition of Black feminist themes. Overall, pop culture and online   media becomes a means through which today's `digital native' student can   develop an awareness of African American literature and Black feminist   thought while acquiring technical skillsets related to data analytics.",
